{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "corpus": "hybrid",
  "class": "hybrid",
  "why": "Almost no real document is 100% one thing. The common cases are a human draft with AI-expanded sections, an AI draft a person edited down, and a document two authors touched in alternation. A detector that only answers 'AI' or 'human' cannot describe any of these, and a detector that answers '73%' should be right about the 73.",
  "construction": "Each hybrid is built by scripts/build-corpus.ts from one human sample and one AI sample in the same domain, spliced at sentence boundaries. Because the splice is mechanical, the ground-truth AI fraction is exact rather than estimated: aiFraction = (words contributed by the AI source) / (total words). Sentence-level provenance is recorded per sample so sentence-granular detectors can be scored on localisation as well as on the document total.",
  "noSmoothing": "The splice does not rewrite the seam. Adding transition sentences would mean writing new text of unknown provenance into a ground-truth corpus. Seams are therefore visible to a careful human reader, which is the honest version of this test: a detector that still cannot localise them has no localisation.",
  "ratios": [0.25, 0.5, 0.75],
  "ratioTolerance": 0.04,
  "modes": {
    "human-draft-ai-expanded": "Human opening and close, one contiguous AI block inserted in the middle. Models the 'I wrote it, I asked a model to flesh out section 2' case.",
    "ai-draft-human-edited": "AI throughout with human sentences replacing a contiguous run. Models the 'model drafted it, I rewrote the parts that were wrong' case.",
    "alternating": "Sentence-level interleaving on a fixed stride. The hardest localisation case and the least common in the wild; included as the upper bound of difficulty."
  },
